has allowed piracy to flourish off the Somali coast, disrupting international shipping routes and
prompting military responses the US and UK.
International efforts to stabilise Somalia, including peacekeeping missions by the African Union,
have had limited success due to ongoing insurgencies, corruption, and clan-based conflicts.
While there have been recent attempts to build functioning institutions, Somalia remains one of
the world’s most fragile states, with persistent violence, underdevelopment, and a humanitarian
crisis affecting millions of its citizens.
Case Study: Yemen
Yemen has descended into a failed state due to years of civil war, economic collapse, and
humanitarian catastrophe. The conflict, which began in 2015 when Houthi rebels overthrew the
government, has resulted in the fragmentation of the country, with multiple factions controlling
different regions. The Yemeni government, backed by a Saudi-led coalition, controls parts of the
country, while the Houthis and other militant groups such as al-Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula
(AQAP) control other areas. The civil war has devastated Yemen’s infrastructure, left millions
displaced, and triggered widespread famine.
The United Nations has called Yemen the world’s worst humanitarian crisis, with millions of
people in urgent need of food, healthcare, and other essential services. Despite international
efforts to broker peace, the conflict remains unresolved, and Yemen’s collapse has created a
power vacuum that extremist groups have exploited. The country’s instability poses significant
security risks to the region and has prompted ongoing international interventions, particularly
from Saudi Arabia and Iran, further complicating the conflict.
Case Study: Sudan
Sudan has faced significant instability since the overthrow of long-time dictator Omar al-Bashir in
2019. A power-sharing agreement between civilian and military leaders initially offered hope for a
democratic transition. However, this fragile arrangement collapsed in October 2021 when the
military staged a coup, ousting the civilian-led transitional government. The coup was met with
mass protests, which were violently suppressed, leading to increased instability and a deepening
economic crisis, including food shortages and inflation.
In April 2023, intense fighting broke out between the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and the
paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF), led by rival military leaders General Abdel Fattah
al-Burhan and General Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo. The civil war has devastated large parts of
the country, particularly the capital, Khartoum, and displaced millions of people. The violence
continues today, leaving Sudan in a state of collapse with a worsening humanitarian crisis and no
clear path to stability.
International efforts to broker peace, led by the African Union and the United Nations, have
struggled to gain traction. With both military factions refusing to cede power, Sudan remains in a
state of collapse, unable to provide basic services to its population. The humanitarian crisis
continues to worsen, with more than five million people displaced and millions more in urgent
need of food, water, and medical assistance. The conflict has also strained neighbouring
countries like Chad and South Sudan, which are struggling to manage the influx of refugees
fleeing the violence.
